---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { REPO, issueUrl } from '../lib/tree';

const missingNode = issueUrl('Missing node: ', 'Factor it belongs under:\n\nWhat would count as it having happened (the resolution criterion):\n\nWho or what would settle it (the source):\n\nWhy the tree needs it:\n');
const moveNumber = issueUrl('Move a probability: ', 'Node id:\n\nThe number I would move, from what to what, and under which scenario:\n\nWhy:\n');
const challenge = issueUrl('Challenge a criterion: ', 'Node id:\n\nWhat is wrong with the resolution criterion, or the source, or a dependency:\n\nWhat I would write instead:\n');
---

<Base title="Feedback — The Belt Equation" description="How to name a missing event, question a test, or suggest a probability move, and how feedback gets folded into the tree.">
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Feedback</p>
    <h1>The point of publishing the tree is that you can improve a specific piece of it</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Three kinds of feedback move the project: an event that is missing, a test that would not settle
      the question, and a number that is too high or too low. Each one is welcome, and each one leaves a trace.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section narrow prose">
    <h2>How to send it</h2>
    <p>
      Feedback goes through issues in the public repository, because issues are durable and quotable, and
      folding one into the tree is a commit that can cite it. Every event's page has a link that opens an issue
      about it with its id already filled in. Or start from one of these (the same three forms are the
      repository's issue templates, so opening a blank issue there offers them too):
    </p>
    <div class="ctas">
      <a class="btn" href={missingNode} rel="noopener">Name a missing event</a>
      <a class="btn ghost" href={moveNumber} rel="noopener">Suggest a number move</a>
      <a class="btn ghost" href={challenge} rel="noopener">Question a test</a>
    </div>
    <p style="margin-top: 18px;">
      Good feedback says which event (the id from its page), what you would change, and why. For a
      missing event, the most useful thing you can give is the test: what would count as it having happened,
      stated so that two people reading it would agree whether it has. An event without a test cannot be
      added.
    </p>

    <h2>What happens to it</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>I rule on it. Some critiques change the tree; some are answered and left as they are. Either way the issue records the ruling.</li>
      <li>A change to the tree lands as a commit that cites the issue, with a dated line in the changelog (<a href="/story/">the story so far</a>).</li>
      <li>A moved probability gets a revision entry on the event, with the old number, the new one, the date, and the reason, so the event's own page shows how it has moved.</li>
      <li>Critique that arrives between reviews is folded in before the next annual review each January, when every event is re-estimated and the numbers are re-run.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="dim">
      A plain issue with the event's id and your reasoning is exactly enough. The repository is at
      <a href={REPO} rel="noopener">github.com/jfredson/belt-equation</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
